This paper investigates a Nirenberg-type problem defined on the standard half-sphere $(\mathbb{S}^n_+, g_0)$. The objective is to determine positive solutions of the following boundary value problem:
$$
\left\{
\begin{array}{lll}
-\Delta_{g_0} u_n = n u_n^{p} & \text{on } \mathbb{S}^n_+, \\
u_n = 0 & \text{on } \partial\mathbb{S}^n_+.
\end{array}
\right.
$$
For $p$ in the subcritical range, we derive these solutions by approximating them using a sequence that converges as $n \to \infty$. Specifically, we examine a family of even extensions of finite-energy solutions previously identified by the second and third authors for problems on the entire sphere $\mathbb{S}^n$. This family is characterized by specific blow-up patterns: solutions consist of exactly two points of multiple blow-up, combining both interior and boundary occurrences, alongside a distinct nonlinearity concentration phenomenon. Consequently, we establish the existence of positive solutions for the original problem on the half-sphere when $1 < p < \frac{n+1}{n-1}$.
